<b>ג.</b> “A little [supplication]…”—our master cites “whether one increases or decreases [his prayers], provided his heart directs his supplications”—for a little with kavanah is better than much without. If at first both increase with kavanah, why equate them with one who decreases? If without kavanah, why then say “better”? Gemara end of Menachot: “whether one increases or decreases, provided his heart is toward Heaven.” Likely as Shabbat 10b—Rava saw Rav Hamnuna prolong prayer and said they abandon eternal life for temporal life [Torah study]; Rav Hamnuna held prayer time is distinct… So “one who increases prayer” is like Rav Hamnuna; “one who decreases” is like Rava whose intent is Torah—and it concludes “provided his heart is toward Heaven,” meaning his shortening is for Heaven’s sake. Thus sometimes lengthening prayer prevents kavanah—it is better to shorten with intent—therefore “whether one increases or decreases” for the sake of kavanah, ending “provided…” meaning lessening is to enable heart toward Heaven; better little with kavanah than much without—that is why one shortens—to gain kavanah—sound.
